v 2.9.3, October 2026 - outputs overwritten in place, safe in synchronized folders (Proton Drive, iCloud, OneDrive); failed workflow steps keep their outputs:
- outputs of a former run that a script writes again are overwritten in place, and those it does not write again are removed at the end of the run (formerly all were removed first): in folders synchronized by Proton Drive, iCloud or OneDrive, deleting a file and writing it again at once made "name 2" copies and lost the originals; when a script stops on an error, the outputs of its former run are kept
- umd_workflow.py: a step that fails keeps the files of its former run (formerly removed) and runs again the next time
- umd2out.py: writes the DeepMD sets into the existing set folders

v 2.9.4, October 2026 - LAMMPS parser checked on real runs; files without forces, stresses or velocities handled:
- LAMMPSParser2umd.py checked on a real LAMMPS run (DeePMD water + NaCl, NPT): KineticEnergyIons = TotEng - PotEng when the thermo output has no KinEng; the pair_coeff command is found also when the deepmd pair style prints it on the line of its info
- files without forces or stresses (e.g. LAMMPS dumps without fx fy fz, logs without Pxx...): check_umd.py skips the net-force and stress checks, viscosity_umd.py stops with an explanation (it gave 0), umd2out.py writes no force.npy in the DeepMD sets
- LAMMPSParser2umd.py checked on a second set (pyrolite melt, dump without id, velocities or forces, with a stress file -a): it reports the steps of the stress file and warns when frames have no line there (their stress was 0 silently), notes that a dump without id must keep the order of the atoms, and says when the dump has no velocities; check_umd.py skips the jump, time-step and temperature checks without velocities, and the energy drift and equilibration of fields 0 in every snapshot

v 2.10, November 2026 - ABINIT parser; a void in build_umd.py; LAMMPS NPT runs unwrapped in reduced coordinates:
- AbinitParser.py (new): ABINIT molecular dynamics output -> UMD, in the layout of ABINIT 6.4.1 (checked on an ionmov 9 run, spin polarized, non-orthogonal cell); other values of ionmov are flagged, variable cells and several datasets are refused
- check_umd.py: the smearing check is skipped when ElectronicEntropy is 0 in every snapshot (it reported OK)
- build_umd.py: --vacuum adds a void along x, y, z (the vectors a, b, c), independently, each with its own thickness, which the script asks for when it is not on the command line
- LAMMPSParser2umd.py checked on two more real runs, with velocities, forces and full thermo output. With a changing box (NPT) the unwrapped positions no longer follow xu, in which an atom at rest moves with the box, more the more cells it crossed; parse NPT runs again. Masses from the log also for dumps without a type column
- check_umd.py: the unwrapping check accepts motion in reduced coordinates when the cell changes, the jump check allows for the move with the cell, the energy drift says when EnergyWithDrift is only the total energy (no econserve in the LAMMPS thermo output)

v 2.11, November 2026 - the graphical interface set aside:
- The graphical interface (UMD_GUI.py, averages_forGUI.py, val_extract_umd.py) is set aside in the gui folder: it no longer ran (it imported scripts replaced during the cleanup) and is not maintained

v 2.12, November 2026 - a validation suite; two corrections found by it:
- validation/ (new): a validation suite. run_validation.py runs every script, with its main options, on the reference runs of data/ (VASP, ABINIT and LAMMPS outputs parsed; the workflow on glycine, pyrolite, ZnS, corundum, hydrous andesite and on small odd files; builds, conversions, speciation, transport and thermodynamics options) and on synthetic files with exact answers (ideal gas, fcc in two cells, rigid water, random walks with a D tensor, Ornstein-Uhlenbeck velocities, harmonic oscillators, Ornstein-Uhlenbeck stresses, harmonic wells for thermodynamic integration, injected faults). It compares key numbers and checksums of every output with the references of validation/reference/, and the synthetic results with their exact values
- averages.py -a: a UMD file without Pressure, Temperature or InternalEnergy (positions only) is skipped with a message; it stopped the run (a traceback, or no fullthermo.dat for the other files)
- vdos_thermo.py: the warning on negative parts of the vDOS advises smoothing or a longer window when the Hann taper was already used

v 2.13, November 2026 - faster speciation angles, bond lifetimes, averages and reading of the atom lines:
- speciation_and_angles.py -t 1: the angles of a snapshot are computed in one pass (formerly one central atom at a time) and written in one block, and the angle file has the angles with 2 decimals (formerly 17 digits; the means keep all their digits): 4.5 times faster (ZnS 72 s to 16 s), the angle file 40 % smaller; the other outputs identical
- speciation_and_angles.py -l 1: the bond lifetimes are computed in numpy (ZnS 2.5 s to 0.7 s); output identical
- check_umd.py, umd2out.py: the atom lines are read by numpy's text parser (np.loadtxt), 1.7 times faster; outputs identical
- averages.py: the fields are read in one pass over the file in Python (formerly one grep per field, and grep is not on Windows): -a 4 times faster (hydrous andesite 10.3 s to 2.8 s); outputs identical

v 2.14, November 2026 - snapshots in batches sized from their bytes, read by the workers:
- Snapshots are given to the parallel workers in batches sized from the bytes of a snapshot in the file (many snapshots per task for small systems, one for big ones), at most two batches per worker at a time, and the workers read their own snapshots from the file (an index of the byte offset of every snapshot, files above 2 GB included): the memory holds a few batches whatever the length of the run, and the results are written as they come
- bonding_umd.py: the bonds of a snapshot are sorted and written in C, by the workers: hydrous andesite 8.8 s to 1.3 s, memory 770 MB to 77 MB; output identical
- speciation_and_angles.py: the clusters, angles, the step file and the bond lifetimes are computed by batches in the workers: ZnS -r 1 -t 1 -l 1 16 s to 6.8 s, hydrous andesite -r 0 5.3 s to 2.4 s; outputs identical
- gofr_umd.py: processes instead of threads (the C reader spent its time on a system lock in threads), each reading its block from the file: hydrous andesite 1.65 s to 1.0 s, memory 307 MB to 65 MB; output identical for the same -k

v 3.0, November 2026 - stable production version: the package cleaned, corrected and checked script by script (2.7 to 2.14), the graphical interface set aside, a validation suite, parallel processing on CPUs:
- every script rewritten or checked for any cell shape and for NPT runs, with errors on the statistical results (see 2.7 to 2.14)
- the graphical interface is set aside in the gui folder (2.11)
- validation/: every script checked on reference runs and on synthetic runs with exact answers (2.12)
- parallel processing on CPUs: snapshots given to worker processes in batches sized from their bytes, read by the workers from the file (2.14)
- ROADMAP: the plan of the v3.Y versions, towards systems of up to 1 billion atoms

v 3.0.1, November 2026 - CP2K parser:
- CP2KParser.py (new): CP2K molecular dynamics run -> UMD. CP2K writes a run in several files, matched here by their MD step: positions (<project>-pos-1.xyz, XMOL or extended XYZ), and when given velocities (-v), forces (-r), energies (-e, the .ener file), stress tensor (-a, the .stress file: total pressure tensor, kinetic part included, as the UMD StressTensor) and cell. The xyz files of CP2K have no cell: -c takes numbers, the .cell file (the cell of every step, NPT), an input or restart file (&CELL section, and the MASS of the &KIND sections), or the main output. With the standard names only -f is needed. Masses other than the standard ones (deuterium) with -m: the parser compares the kinetic energy of the velocities with that of the energy file and prints the masses that fit when they differ. A run restarted from an earlier step, appended to the same files: of the steps written several times the last ones are kept. Unwrapped positions in reduced coordinates for a changing cell, as LAMMPSParser2umd.py. Checked on one real run (100 H2O + 2 H2SO4, NVT, 1001 steps, CP2K 2023.2; cell from the MD_INI lines of its main output): every value of the UMD file equals the CP2K files to its rounding; the cell file, the input, output and extended XYZ layouts, and NPT were checked on files written for the tests, not yet on real ones

v 3.1, November 2026 - orientations and rotational entropy of molecular units:
- rotations_umd.py (new): orientations of the molecular units of a run, a central atom with its bonded neighbours from the bonding file (-c O -a H water, -c N -a H ammonia, -c C -a H methane; -x the number of neighbours, default the most frequent composition; a snapshot counts only with exactly this composition). Orientation by the dipole axis (the sum of the bond vectors: H2O, NH3, OH) or, for units without a dipole (CH4, SO4, CO3), by the bonds, chosen automatically (-u). The correlation of order l between two units is the mean of P_l between their vectors: for dipoles the cosine of their angle (l = 1) and P2; for bonds the orders that the shape of the unit allows (a tetrahedron: 3 and 4). Outputs per unit: correlations between two units against their distance, with their g(r), the dipolar term and the Kirkwood factor (.rot.pair.dat); distribution of the angle between the dipoles of first neighbours, of all and of the linked ones, where a bond of one unit points to the other, i.e. the hydrogen bond (.rot.shell.dat); correlations in time of the orientation of a unit, of its bonds followed atom by atom, and of the total orientation of all the units (.rot.time.dat); map of the orientations on the sphere and its entropy relative to free rotation, with a check of its sampling (.rot.map.dat); summary (.rot.dat). A unit that loses its composition for less than -g fs (default 50) goes on as the same molecule; -m minimum life. Any cell, NPT; errors from blocks. Checked on synthetic runs with exact answers (rotational diffusion of H2O and CH4, pairs with parallel, antiparallel, identical and inverted orientations, jumps between six directions) and on ice VII, liquid water, glycine and a hydrous melt. Entropy of the units by the two-phase thermodynamic model (2PT; Lin, Maiti, Goddard 2010), when the UMD file has velocities: densities of states of the translation of the centre of mass, of the rotation (angular velocities in a body frame followed by fitting the shape of the unit at the start of its life, weighted by the moments of inertia; for linear units in the frame of the cell) and of the internal motions; translation and rotation each with its fluidicity, split into gas-like parts (hard spheres; rigid rotor, with the symmetry number found from the shapes of the units, -y to set it) and solid-like parts (quantum oscillators) (.rot.2pt.dat; entropies in kB and J/(mol K) with block errors in .rot.dat, with the kinetic temperatures, moments of inertia, entropy of the free rotor and the change for 10 % more volume). Volume of a unit (-V): a number, Bader volumes per element (-V O:16.5,H:4.2), or the ACF.dat files of the Bader program for snapshots of the run, matched to their snapshot (its number in the name of the file or of its folder, counted from 0 as umd2out.py) by the positions of the atoms in any order, A or bohr, any origin, so that the Bader analysis can come from any code; default the cell volume per unit, with a warning when other atoms are present. -t temperature. Checked on synthetic rigid rotors (H2O, CH4, OH) with Ornstein-Uhlenbeck translation and rotation: fluidicities equal to the exact ones to 1e-8, entropies to 1e-3, symmetry numbers 2, 12, 1, Bader volumes exact; ice VII (f 0.002 and 0.03) and liquid water (f 0.30 and 0.05)

v 3.2, November 2026 - an example run and its tutorial, towards Windows:
- examples/: an example run, a pyrolite melt at 3000 K (153 atoms, 3 ps of ab initio molecular dynamics), as a UMD file attached to the GitHub release "examples" (its link does not change from one version to the next), with a tutorial and the results to expect (examples/README.md)
- averages.py -p: works without the grep command (Windows): the lines of the field are then read in Python, same output; grep is still used where it exists (faster)
- documentation/umd-quick-help.pdf: title MAGMATOMIX/UMD, footer with the GitHub link, the license and the citation
- documentation/website/umd-package.html: the page of the package on razvancaracas.info (description, the UMD format, installation, tutorial)
- ROADMAP: the large-system steps renumbered v3.3 to v3.7

v 3.3, November 2026 - compositional fluctuations, exchange between a melt and its gas, compressed runs:
- composition_umd.py (new): compositional fluctuations. The cell is cut into a grid of sub-cells in reduced coordinates (-n na,nb,nc, or -l the length of a sub-cell, default 5 A; any cell, NPT: every sub-cell has the volume V/n at every snapshot) and the atoms of each element are counted in each sub-cell at every snapshot, read in batches by worker processes. Outputs, all in the folder <base>.comp.<grid> (one per grid, so that several grids live side by side; removed at once with the folder or by umd_clean.py): distributions of the number of atoms of each element in a sub-cell with the binomial law of ideal mixing, and of the local density (dist.dat, density.dat); for each element the variance of its count divided by the binomial one (1 ideal gas, < 1 dense liquid, > 1 when the element gathers) and the correlation of the counts of every pair of elements, with errors (summary.dat); voids, the sub-cells whose density of melt elements (all but the volatiles -g, default those of H, He, C, N, F, Ne, S, Cl, Ar, Kr, Xe present) is below -d (default 0.2) times the density of the melt, connected across the periodic faces: bubbles in a melt, or the gas around a droplet in an expanded run, with the dense regions, their densities, and the fraction of the atoms of each element in the voids and in the largest dense region, i.e. the partition between gas and condensed phase (voids.dat, per snapshot); correlations in time of the local composition, of the voids and of the density, and the times over which they are kept (time.dat). -c follows sub-cells one by one (-c 1:1:1,5:2:2; r one at random, r5 five, all): the table subcells.dat has one line per sub-cell of the run (mean number of atoms of each element with errors, mole fractions and their enrichment relative to the whole cell, fraction of the time it is a void, density, how long its content is kept; with -c all the map of the composition), and each sub-cell its file subcell_<ia>_<ib>_<ic>.dat (per snapshot the number of atoms of each element, their mole fractions, the density and whether it is a void, its means in the header; the files of other sub-cells, from former runs, are kept; a warning beyond 125 sub-cells); -a the elements followed in these files and in the plot (e.g. -a Si,O); -v plots the sub-cells of -c (without -c every sub-cell in random order): the number of atoms of each element against time, stacked, and the density of the sub-cell with the void threshold and the voids shaded; Previous and Next buttons. Checked on synthetic runs with exact answers (ideal mixing: variance ratio 1, correlation 0, time correlation 0.9^t, errors calibrated over 8 runs; segregated halves: ratio 2; a hole across the periodic faces: voids, regions, densities and partition exact) and on pyrolite + H2 at 4000 K expanded to 0.2 g/cm3 (a droplet holding about 80 % of the melt atoms, about 70 % of the H in the gas). For alloys and any dense system: the concentration fluctuation ratio of each element, the variance of N_X - x_X N_all in a sub-cell divided by its exact value for a random alloy given the atoms of each sub-cell (hypergeometric): 1 random, > 1 segregation or demixing (e.g. Ni in undercooled Fe-Ni), < 1 chemical order; density fluctuations do not enter it; with the means of the four quarters of the run, each with its error, and a warning when two quarters differ by more than 4 errors (segregation or mixing in progress), and per snapshot in fluctuations.dat. States of the sub-cells and how long they last (episodes.dat and summary): void, dense, and composition states -e Ni:1.5 (enriched, x >= 1.5 times that of the cell) or Ni:0.5 (depleted), with the fraction of the time expected for a random alloy; durations of the complete periods (those cut by the start or the end of the run counted apart), mean with error; per sub-cell in subcells.dat. Checked on a synthetic Fe-Ni (random: concentration ratio 0.997 +/- 0.016, enriched fraction 0.0963 against 0.0964 exact; Ni gathered in a third of the cell in the second half: quarters 1.00, 0.99, 15.37, 15.39 against 1, 1, 15.40, 15.40, change detected) and a hole opening for 30 fs and closing for 20 fs (periods exact). Heterogeneities that stay (summary): for each element, the voids and the density, the persistent fraction, the part of the variance that stays in place over the run (the mean of each sub-cell squared, minus its noise 2 tau/T x its variance, over the total variance; 0 when the sub-cells are alike on average), and the 1/e time of the fluctuations of each sub-cell about its own mean (the bias of removing a finite mean corrected), which decay even when the heterogeneity stays, besides the 1/e time of the whole correlation (not reached when the heterogeneity stays) and its value at the end. Checked on sub-cells of two fixed weights with atoms drawn anew (persistent fraction 0.414 exact: over 8 runs unbiased, errors calibrated; fluctuations 0.9^t: 9.51 fs on average against 9.49); pyrolite + H2: 65 % of the density pattern persists over the 2.2 ps (the droplet stays), H only 2.5 % (mixed in 62 fs), the local fluctuations decay in 250-390 fs. Bubbles and droplets followed in time (regions.dat and summary): the connected void and dense regions of every snapshot are followed by the sub-cells they share (the largest overlaps first): births (new, or by the split of a region), deaths (vanished, or merged into a region), lifetimes, volume and atoms of each element of every region; regions living less than -t are fluctuations of the surface, counted apart. Checked on bubbles of a scripted history (lives of 100, 250, 220 fs, a merge, a split, regions cut by the start and the end: exact); pyrolite + H2: the gas one region all along, the droplet shedding small clusters (~2.4 O, 1.2 Mg, 0.8 Si, 1.5 H) that live 188 +/- 25 fs, 30 born by a split, 25 merging back. Earlier attempts, never in the package: bubbles.py (2017) and densitytracker.py (T. Bogels, 2020)
- compressed runs: every script that reads a UMD file also takes <run>.umd.dat.gz. It is decompressed into a temporary file (in the folder of the system for temporary files, or TMPDIR: never next to the run, which may be in a synchronized folder), read as usual, and removed at the end of the script, also after an error or when the script is killed; the .gz is never changed, and the outputs have their usual names (<run>.gofr.dat, ...), next to the .gz. -f all takes the *.umd.dat.gz files too (the uncompressed file when a run is there in both forms), one at a time; averages.py too; thermo_integration_umd.py and umd_workflow.py decompress each run once for the whole run (the steps of the workflow read the same temporary file, and a step is done before when its outputs are newer than the .gz). Outputs identical to those of the uncompressed run (checked for 17 scripts, the workflow and the thermodynamic integration); umd_clean.py recognizes the outputs of compressed runs
- exchange_umd.py (new; the exchange part of composition_umd.py, moved): exchange of atoms between a condensed phase (melt, droplet, slab) and the gas, through their interface. The condensed phase is defined by the smoothed density of its elements (all but the volatiles -g), as the instantaneous surface of Willard and Chandler (2010): Gaussians of width -q (2.4 A) on a grid (-p 1 A, any cell, NPT), the surface at half the bulk density (measured at grid points deep inside, or -r). Every atom is condensed (L), in the interface (I: the surface within -x of it, default 3.1 A, where a flat surface is 0.1 and 0.9 of the bulk; a distance, because the smoothed density fluctuates by ~7 % in the bulk of a melt) or in the gas (G). Paths through the interface: G-I-G (a gas atom bouncing back), G-I-L (condensation), L-I-L (a melt atom visiting the surface), L-I-G (evaporation), with the time spent in the interface, the sticking coefficient and the escape probability (paths.dat). Exchanges: evaporations and condensations, rate constants, stays in each phase (stays.dat, atoms.dat), fluxes in both directions per time window (-w) and the time from which they are equal, i.e. equilibrium (fluxes.dat); stays shorter than -t (50 fs) between two equal states merged. Interface area (coarea of the density) and volumes per snapshot (states.dat). Gas: temperature (kinetic; snapshots with faulty velocities left out), composition, partial pressures n k T, partition coefficients between the condensed phase and the gas, the Hertz-Knudsen flux on the interface against the counted arrivals and the condensation coefficient (gas.dat). Molecules, from a bonding file of bonding_umd.py (-b, default <base>.bonding.dat): the gas by molecule (composition, partial pressures) and the evaporations and condensations by molecule (molecules.dat); -m clusters: the condensed phase as the molecules of at least -l atoms (<base>.exchange.clusters). Outputs in the folder <base>.exchange. Checked on synthetic runs with exact answers: He jumping between an Ar slab and the gas (rates, stays, gas fraction, interface 8 nm^2, bulk density, temperature, pressure, partition, Hertz-Knudsen flux), He scripted through the interface (passages and times exact), He degassing (fluxes of every window), H2 and He with bonds (molecular events and pressure exactly half those of the H atoms; -m clusters); pyrolite + H2 at 4000 K: the gas mostly H2 (51 %), H, H2O, OH, SiO2H2, SiO, pressure of the molecules 0.10 GPa; hydrous andesite: a bulk melt, all condensed
- liquid_and_vapor_speciation.py merged into exchange_umd.py: exchange_umd.py -P <popul file> -S <r0 step file> -l <length> writes the same files (byte-identical on glycine); lifetimes.py -l uses it
- ROADMAP: the large-system steps renumbered v3.4 to v3.8

v 3.3.1, November 2026 - the workflow runs the compositional fluctuations and the melt-gas exchange:
- umd_workflow.py: the transport panel (-t) also runs composition_umd.py (sub-cells of about 5 A; not for cells narrower than 7.5 A), and exchange_umd.py when the run has a gas phase (mean void fraction of composition_umd.py at least 0.1) and a condensed phase (at least 10 atoms of the elements that are not volatile), with the molecules of the bonding file when there is one. The summary has the void fraction, and for runs with a gas the interface area, gas volume fraction, gas temperature and the pressures of the atoms and of the molecules in the gas

v 3.3.2, November 2026 - gas pressure by the modified Van der Waals law, adsorbed atoms:
- exchange_umd.py: pressure of the gas by molecules with the modified Van der Waals law of Davis and Caracas (2024, Commun. Earth Environ. 5, 344), P = N k T/V - a N^2/(NA^2 V^2), a of each species from the CRC Handbook (H2, He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe, N2, O2, CO, CO2, H2O, CH4, NH3, H2S, SO2, HCl, Cl2, F2, NO, N2O, NO2, HF, HCN, C2H6, SiH4, Hg; -A for others; species without a counted as ideal and listed), mixture a N^2 = (sum N_i a_i^1/2)^2. The volume of the gas from the smoothed density, no Bader analysis needed, with two definitions side by side: the gas zone (all the density within -x below half the bulk) and the volume below the half-density surface; a molecule is gas when all its atoms are; atoms outside whose molecule is not (bonded to the condensed phase, even by one atom) are adsorbed, not gas. T = the kinetic temperature of the atoms of the gas molecules. Per snapshot (pressure.dat) and averaged over the run with errors from the autocorrelation time (summary.dat); molecules.dat also gives the number of each molecule below the surface and its a. Checked on a synthetic run (H2 and He between an Ar slab and the gas, and a Xe in the gas bonded to the slab): gas molecules and adsorbed Xe exact in every snapshot, pressure within 3e-6. pyrolite + H2 at 4000 K: 0.097 +/- 0.002 GPa (gas zone), 0.092 +/- 0.002 GPa (below the surface); the attraction lowers it by 0.2 %
- umd_workflow.py: the summary has the two pressures of the gas molecules
- exchange_umd.py with a bonding file: an atom in the gas zone whose molecule is not all in it (bonded to the condensed phase, even by one atom) is adsorbed and counts in the interface for everything (time in each state, paths, evaporations and condensations, gas composition and temperature): an atom evaporates when its molecule detaches. Without a bonding file nothing changes. pyrolite + H2: H in the gas 76 % -> 69 % of the time, sticking coefficient of H 0.036 -> 0.026, gas temperature 4125 -> 4059 K, ideal pressure of the gas atoms 0.30 -> 0.26 GPa; synthetic check: a Xe in the gas bonded to the slab is never gas and never evaporates

v 3.4, November 2026 - native Windows, tests on three systems, exchange between two condensed phases:
- Windows: the package runs natively on Windows 10 and 11 (WINDOWS.md: Python, the package, the compiled libraries, the tutorial). bonding_umd.py found its C library by splitting the path of the script on '/', which failed on Windows (now os.path, as the other scripts)
- src/compile_libraries.py (new): compiles the four C libraries on macOS (.dylib), Linux (.so) or Windows (.dll, gcc of MinGW-w64, without dependence on its DLLs)
- tests on GitHub (GitHub Actions, .github/workflows/tests.yml): at every change, the libraries compiled and the synthetic cases of the validation suite run on Linux, Windows and macOS; every release gets the compiled libraries of the three systems attached (umd-c-libraries-windows.zip, -linux.zip, -macos.zip)
- validation: syn_workflow, the whole workflow on a synthetic run (no data needed, so it runs on GitHub too); output names compared with / on every system
- ROADMAP: native Windows is v3.4; the large-system steps renumbered v3.5 to v3.9
- documentation/umd-flowchart: rotations_umd.py (structural panel), the gas pressure of exchange_umd.py, compressed runs (.umd.dat.gz), umd_clean.py
- exchange_umd.py -c <elements of phase A>: exchange between two condensed phases (e.g. -c Fe: He between liquid iron and a silicate melt). The field is the smoothed density of the elements of A only: phase A where it is above half its bulk, phase B the rest, whatever its density (without -c both liquids are dense and everything is condensed). The same states, paths, exchanges and fluxes, labelled A and B; instead of the gas, the partition between the zones of A and B: number densities (D), mole fractions (K) and mass fractions (D_mass, the metal/silicate partition coefficient), with errors. Gas options (-g, -b, -m clusters, -T, -A) refused with -c. Outputs in the folder <base>.exchange.<elements of A>. Checked on a synthetic run (He between an Fe slab and a Si-O slab of the same density, O dissolved in the Fe and Fe in the Si-O; validation case syn_twophase): rates, stays, D within their errors, K and D_mass exact, the dissolved atoms in their host; outputs of the gas mode unchanged

Since v 3.4 (not yet released):
- msrd_umd.py (new): mean square relative displacements (MSRD) of the bonds of coordination polyhedra (-c Si -a O: SiO4; -x the number of adjacent atoms, default the most frequent composition), from the bonding file. For each bond, b = r_adjacent - r_central (the translation of the polyhedron cancels in it), MSRD(t) = <[(b(t0+t) - b(t0)).e(t0)]^2> over the bonds and all the time origins (FFT), e(t0) along the bond (parallel) or across it: perp1 in the plane of the bond and of the second central atom of a bridging adjacent atom (the Si-O-Si plane), else of the nearest other adjacent atom (the O-Si-O plane), perp2 normal to that plane; bridging and non-bridging bonds apart, by their state at t0. Two frames: the cell (lab: the transverse MSRD includes the rotation of the polyhedron) and the polyhedron (body: its rigid rotation between t0 and t0+t removed by the mass-weighted Kabsch fit). A polyhedron is followed by its central atom while it keeps the same adjacent atoms; gaps of at most -g fs without its composition bridged. Static values, as measured by EXAFS: mean bond length, its variance (the Debye-Waller factor of the distance) and third cumulant; sigma2 parallel and perpendicular about the mean positions of each life, the distance R0 between the mean positions (<r> - R0 = sigma2_perp/(2 R0) checked: pyrolite SiO4 0.0283 and 0.0284 A in the body frame). Errors from blocks. Outputs <base>.<unit>.msrd.time.dat and .msrd.dat. -v plots them (rows lab and body frames, columns all, bridging and non-bridging bonds; the MSRD along and across the bonds with their error bands, the static 2 sigma2 plateaus dotted, the static values in text boxes; one page per adjacent element, Previous/Next), -p writes the plot to <base>.<unit>.msrd.pdf. Checked on a synthetic run (validation case syn_msrd: SiO4 and Si2O7 turning, drifting and vibrating in a triclinic cell deformed at every snapshot, with a bridged gap and a broken life): every MSRD and static value equal to a brute-force computation (body frame by Horn's quaternions) to 3e-9
- umd_workflow.py runs the basic analyses only (faster, and a clear line between a basic and an advanced user): speciation_and_angles.py -r 0 (polymerization, molecules), msd_clusters_umd.py and analyze_msd_clusters.py are no longer steps of the workflow; they are advanced analyses, run by hand. The cluster MSDs were not usable as the workflow ran them: of the 54 r1 species of the reference runs (pyrolite 33, glycine 7, corundum 3, ZnS 3...), none reached the diffusive regime (alpha 0.8-1.2; all 33 of pyrolite above 1.5, still ballistic), because most coordination species live less than the time a melt takes to become diffusive; run them by hand for long-lived molecules, with a chosen fit window
- documentation/umd-flowchart: msrd_umd.py (transport panel); the advanced analyses, run by hand and not by umd_workflow.py (rotations_umd.py, msrd_umd.py, msd_clusters_umd.py, liquid/vapor partitioning), in dashed boxes and lines; the label of each panel says what the workflow runs. Marked advanced in the README and the quick help
